  "query_preview": "/TONIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 338 PM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\n===== Sunday ======\n\nWe remain in deeper NW flow on Sunday as the strong upper ridging \nsits over the western US and a trough to the east. Strong daytime \nheating, and slightly more humidity, will support strong \ndestabilization during the day, potentially hitting 3000-4000 J/kg \nof SBCAPE. Overall flow through the column remains somewhat weak, \nespecially below 500mb, but sfc-6km shear parameters over 30kts does \nindicate a more favorable profile for organized storms. The biggest \nuncertainty continues to be if we get any trigger to initiate \nconvection. If we are able to achieve convective initiation, some \nsevere storms may be possible with mainly a wind threat as DCAPEs \nover 1000 J/kg may be possible. But if we do not, then the bigger \nfocus will be on the building heat and humidity. Highs on Sunday are \nforecast to hit the lower 90s, with heat indices reaching the upper \n90s and close to 100 assuming no afternoon convection. \n\nThe GFS and NAM continue to indicate some convective system diving \nsouth through the Ohio Valley Sunday night, but the ECMWF and \nCanadian continue to keep us dry. Will be watched closely as we \nbegin to get into more CAM resolution. \n\n===== Next Week =====\n\nThe NW flow pattern holds strong through Monday and Tuesday as well \nas the upper ridge remains locked over the western US. We'll \ncontinue to see increasing heat and humidity during this time, with \ntemps forecast to reach the lower 90s, and perhaps some mid 90s as \nwell. Sfc dewpoints returning to the 70s will ultimately yield heat \nindices on either side of 100, which could lead to needing some heat \nheadlines next week if the forecast plays out. The one caveat to \nthat will be the convective outlook for next week, which the \ntemperature forecast could be upended if we see any MCS activity. \n\nAddressing the convective potential for next week, a stronger mid-\nlevel shortwave is expected to pivot across the Great Lakes on \nMonday, and could be a trigger for MCS activity to develop upstream \nof the NW flow. This NW flow would place the Ohio Valley in the path \nof any MCS activity, which could include the northern portion of the \nforecast area. SPC continues with a 15% risk in the D4 outlook, and \nwould not be surprised to see a portion of our area get included in \nan upcoming outlook for the days ahead. The AI-NWP based convective \nhazard guidance is highlighting the lower Great Lakes and Ohio \nValley for severe potential on Monday, and a mid-Atlantic threat on \nTuesday. One limiting factor to the forecast confidence continues to \nthe inconsistency of global guidance output of notable features, but \nwe will continue to monitor for the days ahead.",
